#set heading(numbering: none)

= Syllabus and Overview

#fade[Michaelmas Term, 2025] #h(1fr) #fade[[24 Lectures]]

*Complex Numbers* #h(1fr) #fade[[2 Lectures]]

Review of complex numbers, including complex conjugate, inverse, modulus, argument and Argand diagram. Informal treatment of complex logarithm, $n$-th roots and complex powers. de Moivre’s theorem.

*Vectors* #h(1fr) #fade[[5 Lectures]]

Review of elementary algebra of vectors in $RR^3$, including scalar product. Brief discussion of vectors in $RR^n$ and $CC^n$; scalar product and the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality. Concepts of linear span, linear independence, subspaces, basis and dimension.

*Matrices* #h(1fr) #fade[[8 Lectures]]

Elementary algebra of $3×3$ matrices, including determinants. Extension to n×n complex matrices. Trace, determinant, non-singular matrices and inverses. Matrices as linear transformations; examples of geometrical actions including rotations, reflections, dilations, shears; kernel and image, rank–nullity theorem (statement only). #h(1fr) #fade[[4]]

Simultaneous linear equations: matrix formulation; existence and uniqueness of solutions, geometric interpretation; Gaussian elimination. #h(1fr) #fade[[3]]

Symmetric, anti-symmetric, orthogonal, Hermitian and unitary matrices. Decomposition of a general matrix into isotropic, symmetric trace-free and antisymmetric parts. #h(1fr) #fade[[1]]

*Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors* #h(1fr) #fade[[9 Lectures]]

Eigenvalues and eigenvectors; geometric significance. #h(1fr) #fade[[2]]

Proof that eigenvalues of Hermitian matrix are real, and that distinct eigenvalues give an orthogonal basis of eigenvectors. The effect of a general change of basis (similarity transformations). Diagonalization of general matrices: sufficient conditions; examples of matrices that cannot be diagonalized. Canonical forms for $2×2$ matrices. #h(1fr) #fade[[5]]

Discussion of quadratic forms, including change of basis. Classification of conics, cartesian and polar forms. #h(1fr) #fade[[1]]

Rotation matrices and Lorentz transformations as transformation groups. #h(1fr) #fade[[1]]
